{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>In the morning, and HaShem will know those who are His.</b> Its meaning is not the setting of a time, to say: tomorrow this sign will come about — for if so, it should have said ״בֹּקֶר יֹדַע ה׳״; what is ״וְיֹדַע״ with a vav? It should have said ״אֲשֶׁר אֵלָיו״; what is ״לוֹ״? Rather, this is its meaning: the morning will verify and clarify the judgment — with whom it lies. And this is because Korah disputed the priesthood and the gifts of priesthood, as is found in the Midrash (Yalkut Shimoni, Korach 16:1) in the matter that Korah recounted to the entire congregation: there was a widow who had a sheep, and Aaron took the first of the fleece and the firstborn and the foreleg and the cheeks, etc. And a proof for this is that after the land rested quietly from the dispute, HaShem gave Aaron the gifts as a covenant of salt forever — hear from this that Korah had been protesting against them. And Moses said: By this you shall know that HaShem gave Aaron the gifts in exchange for his service, for anything given in the morning is something given in joy and with a radiant countenance, and this is an indication that the giving is a fitting thing, as Rashi explained in parashat Beshalah (Exodus 16:7) concerning the manna that was given in the morning. And therefore it is said further on regarding the gifts of priesthood: ״וְאָנֹכִי הִנֵּה נָתַתִּי לְךָ״ — Rashi explained: in joy, like ״הִנֵּה הוּא יֹצֵא לִקְרָאתְךָ וְרָאֲךָ וְשָׂמַח בְּלִבּוֹ״ (Exodus 4:14), which means with a radiant countenance. And anything given in joy and with a radiant countenance is given in the morning, in the morning, when He bestows that which was asked fittingly. If so, the morning will clarify that the truth is with Aaron. ״וְיֹדַע ה׳ אֶת אֲשֶׁר לוֹ״ — for to Him, blessed be He, belongs silver and gold, and from what is His He gives to His ministers, those who do His will — and not as Korah said, that he took them without the authority of the King, blessed be He." "And from the fact that Moses said, ״לֹא חֲמוֹר אֶחָד נָשָׂאתִי״ — hear from this that just as they suspected him of giving Aaron the priesthood so that he might gain the gifts of priesthood, so too they suspected Moses, saying: the kingship is his alone, so that he might thereby gain from this the prerogative of kingship: ״אֶת חֲמֹרֵיכֶם יִקָּח״ etc. (I Samuel 8:16). Therefore he said, as one defending himself: not only did I not take by force a single donkey belonging to any one of them, but I did not even take from them by way of gift. This is why he said ״נָשָׂאתִי״ — in the sense of ״וַיִּשָּׂא מַשְׂאֹת מֵאֵת פָּנָיו״ (Genesis 43:34). And because it is the way of kings to do harm to one who does not give them gifts, therefore he said, ״וְלֹא הֲרֵעֹתִי אֶת אַחַד מֵהֶם״. And according to what we explained above, that Korah disputed the very act of appointment [to positions of authority], he said here ״בֹּקֶר״ — for just as you cannot turn morning into evening, by this ״וְיֹדַע ה׳ אֶת אֲשֶׁר לוֹ״ — for to Him, blessed be He, belongs the earth and its fullness, and He is the one who crowns kings, and in His hand, blessed be He, is it to bring near to Himself whomever He chooses."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {1 "Reading בריאה ('act of appointment/creation [of offices]') based on context: the author argues Korah objected not merely to specific priestly gifts but to the entire divinely ordained structure of appointment. The word could also carry its literal sense of 'creation,' suggesting Korah disputed the very created order."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:44.637940615Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}